1
00:00:05,290 --> 00:00:14,160
Hello everyone, welcome back.

2
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:19,080
Today we are tackling one of the most interesting visual tasks in IELTS writing.

3
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,080
Task 1: Maps.

4
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:26,480
If you've ever played spot the difference as a child, you are already halfway there.

5
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:31,420
Map questions ask you to look at a location at two different points in time,

6
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:36,160
like 1986 versus today, and describe the changes.

7
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:39,990
In this lesson we will master three frameworks, 

8
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:42,960
the big three for basic analysis, 

9
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,540
the great three for finding your overview,

10
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:49,220
and the major three for organising your body paragraphs.

11
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:55,060
We will also analyse a band five sample to see exactly what mistakes to avoid.

12
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:56,480
Let's get started.

13
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:05,720
First, let's look at a band five sample for this map.

14
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:14,060
After three sections on four criteria, let's see how we can improve it to band six plus.

15
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:19,520
Please pause the video for five minutes to read the sample and

16
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:24,380
think about what you would learn from this one and what you should avoid.

17
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:31,720
When analysing maps, we also follow three familiar steps, 

18
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:36,480
the big three, the great three, and the major three.

19
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:39,520
Let's go into the big three.

20
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:42,260
One, what and where?

21
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:48,720
This map doesn't have a title, so let's find the what and where from the question.

22
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:55,260
With maps, these two elements are often combined, and here it's the

23
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:56,580
town of Stokeford.

24
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:02,080
The map shows the changes over time of a town called Stokeford.

25
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:04,480
Two, when?

26
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:11,020
The map doesn't have axes, but the time is clearly written directly

27
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:15,780
on the image, which is 1986 to present.

28
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:20,000
From there, you'll find out the verb tense we should use.

29
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:26,360
With maps, you must pay special attention to the features shown on each map, 

30
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:31,800
like what is located where, what is next to what,

31
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:32,420
etc.

32
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:36,300
The verb tense should match the time of the map.

33
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:39,480
1986, past.

34
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:45,560
When describing features in the map 1986, we'll use the past tense.

35
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:47,920
Present is the present.

36
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:54,060
When describing features in the map present, we'll use present tense.

37
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:59,420
Changes from 1986 to present, present perfect.

38
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:06,560
When describing changes between two maps from 1986 to present, we'll use

39
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:07,900
present perfect.

40
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:10,140
Three, unit.

41
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:11,100
None.

42
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:14,540
Maps have no unit, so we'll skip this.

43
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:19,540
We've done the first step quickly and concisely.

44
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:23,440
Let's move on to the great three, including...

45
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:26,180
One, keys.

46
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:35,460
For maps, there is no subject of comparison, but they are replaced by keys, which are symbols on the map.

47
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:38,660
Two, global feature number one.

48
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:43,420
And three, global feature number two.

49
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:50,300
Maps also have no trends, so let's focus on two global features.

50
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:53,020
One, keys, houses.

51
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:57,180
First, the symbol system, or the keys of the map.

52
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:02,140
With this map, most symbols have been directly explained on it.

53
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:05,080
Only houses is noted outside.

54
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:10,560
Please pay attention to changing the word forms of the keys, 

55
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:12,900
if needed, to fit the grammar of the sentence.

56
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,120
Wait a moment.

57
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:19,459
Let's stop for a few minutes before going into global features.

58
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,080
I have a mini challenge for you.

59
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:29,200
Within 10 seconds, can you identify two major changes in your hometown

60
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:31,100
over the past 10 years?

61
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:34,000
And the time starts now.

62
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:50,800
For me, my hometown has changed a lot in the last 10 years.

63
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:58,260
But what impresses me the most, and I think many people also agree with me, is more people.

64
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:05,280
It's getting more crowded, busier and livelier, and more modern facilities.

65
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:10,580
It's becoming more and more modern, with many new facilities.

66
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:15,520
At this point, maps are quite similar to the reality.

67
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:21,260
So, when finding global features for the overview, you should focus on

68
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:22,920
such significant changes.

69
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:28,100
And I suggest you pay attention to five aspects below.

70
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:36,340
Firstly, important areas, such as industrial areas, residential or housing areas, 

71
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:45,060
commercial areas, recreational areas, green space and woodland or forest.

72
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:48,200
Are there more or less of these?

73
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,380
Secondly, population.

74
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:55,180
Is there an increase or a decrease?

75
00:05:55,740 --> 00:05:58,180
Thirdly, size.

76
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:01,800
Is it enlarged or narrowed?

77
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:04,220
Fourthly, transport.

78
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:07,340
Is it being developed or extended?

79
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:10,120
And lastly, infrastructure.

80
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:14,640
Is it being developed or improved or demolished?

81
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:16,820
Two.

82
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:19,500
Global feature number one.

83
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:25,340
Applying the five aspects above to this map, we can see that

84
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:28,100
the size of the town remains unchanged.

85
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:30,960
So, this could be the first global feature.

86
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:32,200
Three.

87
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:34,660
Global feature number two.

88
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:38,820
There are many new houses, which is a noticeable change.

89
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:42,280
So, this information could be included in the overview.

90
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:48,060
More houses have been built, replacing all the farmland to meet growing demand.

91
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:57,380
Let's move on to the major three to search for information  used in the body paragraphs, including, 

92
00:06:57,380 --> 00:07:05,400
One: grouping information. Two: individual features and Three: detail organization.

93
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:10,340
First, let's talk about grouping information.

94
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:13,420
How to divide the two body paragraphs?

95
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:17,700
With maps, I often follow one of the two methods.

96
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:20,920
One, grouping by map.

97
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:28,760
Describe each map in a paragraph, meaning one paragraph for before and the other for after.

98
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:33,460
The advantage of this method is that it's easy to do and

99
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:37,580
suitable for maps with minimal changes and few details.

100
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:43,940
However, for maps with many details, you must be careful to avoid

101
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:46,160
writing too much information.

102
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:50,260
Two, grouping by area of changes.

103
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:55,200
It means we can divide the map into two halves, like 

104
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:59,860
the eastern and western zones, depending on the changes that have occurred there,

105
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:04,080
or group changes with similar features into one paragraph.

106
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:10,100
This method is more complex, but is suitable for maps with numerous changes, 

107
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:14,480
and it's easy to compare two periods with each other.

108
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:20,900
With this map, let's follow method two, grouping by area of changes,

109
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:23,760
as there are a lot of details on the map.

110
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:27,060
And I'll divide the body as follows.

111
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:29,860
One, grouping information.

112
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:34,280
Body one, the main road, the farmland and the houses.

113
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:37,360
Three subjects that change the most.

114
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:40,500
Body two, other facilities.

115
00:08:41,720 --> 00:08:45,340
Now, to identify the details used in the body.

116
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:47,940
Let's spot the difference.

117
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:54,140
Please circle the features that have changed during this period and square

118
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:56,700
the ones that remain unchanged.

119
00:08:57,580 --> 00:09:03,080
Let's do this exercise in two minutes and then we'll analyze it together.

120
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:08,460
It's not difficult at all, right?

121
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:13,020
I guess you have already come up with answers like this.

122
00:11:13,020 --> 00:11:18,200
So let's do another exercise to apply the information we've just found.

123
00:11:18,820 --> 00:11:22,620
There are six features that have changed during this period.

124
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:27,520
We'll go through each feature and you can fill the missing words

125
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:30,540
in the blanks to see how the feature has changed.

126
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:35,900
For each sentence, you'll have 10 seconds to think and fill in the word.

127
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:40,260
Let's start with the first subject, main road.

128
00:11:53,470 --> 00:11:57,570
In 1986, there was a main road running through this town.

129
00:11:58,030 --> 00:12:01,550
So let's fill in blank one with ran.

130
00:12:02,470 --> 00:12:09,210
Now, the road is still there, but has been expanded, branching out to both sides.

131
00:12:09,510 --> 00:12:12,690
So the answer to blank two is both.

132
00:12:13,850 --> 00:12:16,170
Let's continue with farmlands.

133
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:36,340
In 1986, there were two farmlands used to raise cattle in the northeast and the southwest.

134
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:41,420
So let's fill in northeast, southwest and cattle.

135
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:45,800
However, they have disappeared completely.

136
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:51,400
Another feature that changed a lot and closely related to

137
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:54,880
 the two features we've just mentioned is houses.

138
00:13:08,180 --> 00:13:12,120
Previously, there were only a few houses next to the main road.

139
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:17,020
So the first answer is road or the main road.

140
00:13:17,820 --> 00:13:23,220
By the following years, the number of houses increased significantly along the

141
00:13:23,220 --> 00:13:27,600
main road and its branches, replacing the farmlands.

142
00:13:28,380 --> 00:13:29,900
Next is gardens.

143
00:13:43,150 --> 00:13:51,930
In 1986, a large garden was next to or beside the main road, including the large house.

144
00:13:52,650 --> 00:13:58,110
However, at the present, this area has been narrowed while the large house 

145
00:13:58,110 --> 00:14:02,270
has been expanded to become a retirement home.

146
00:14:03,050 --> 00:14:04,150
And how about shops?

147
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:22,640
In 1986, there were two shops next to the post office, 

148
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:24,280
but now they've both disappeared.

149
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:27,320
Finally, primary school.

150
00:14:40,570 --> 00:14:46,230
In 1986, this primary school was located near the main road and the garden.

151
00:14:47,210 --> 00:14:50,850
Over time, it has been expanded or widened.

152
00:14:51,450 --> 00:14:54,770
Let's take a look at the analysis once again.

153
00:14:55,730 --> 00:15:03,750
Main road, 1986, ran across the town and present has branched out to both sides.

154
00:15:04,310 --> 00:15:12,770
Farmlands, 1986, located in the northeast and southwest, used to raise cattle.

155
00:15:13,550 --> 00:15:15,730
Present have disappeared completely.

156
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:22,050
Houses, 1986, only a few next to the road or the main road 

157
00:15:22,050 --> 00:15:24,610
and only in the northern area.

158
00:15:25,210 --> 00:15:29,810
Present number has increased significantly along the main road and its

159
00:15:29,810 --> 00:15:32,150
branches, replacing the farmlands.

160
00:15:32,910 --> 00:15:43,270
Gardens, 1986, they covered a large area next to or beside the main road and accommodated the large house.

161
00:15:43,850 --> 00:15:48,770
Present, they have shrunk, but the large house has been expanded into

162
00:15:48,770 --> 00:15:50,090
a retirement home.

163
00:15:51,010 --> 00:15:57,810
Shops, 1986, there were two next to the post office and in

164
00:15:57,810 --> 00:15:59,710
the present, they have disappeared.

165
00:16:00,530 --> 00:16:07,110
Primary school, 1986, next to the main road near the garden 

166
00:16:07,110 --> 00:16:09,970
and present, still there but has been expanded.

167
00:16:10,930 --> 00:16:16,770
The final step is to organize all the information earlier into

168
00:16:16,770 --> 00:16:20,030
two body paragraphs to make a complete outline.

169
00:16:20,930 --> 00:16:25,670
In each body, you can describe all the features in 1986 first,

170
00:16:26,110 --> 00:16:31,310
then describe how they have changed to the present or 

171
00:16:31,310 --> 00:16:35,910
describe each feature one by one, how it was before and how it is now.

172
00:16:36,770 --> 00:16:41,330
We should apply the first way to avoid repeating the year too much.

173
00:16:41,710 --> 00:16:42,950
And here is the outline.

174
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:52,600
I guess you still remember the band 5 sample we discussed at the beginning, right?

175
00:16:53,340 --> 00:16:57,820
At that time, perhaps it was quite difficult to identify what was

176
00:16:57,820 --> 00:17:02,300
good, what needed improvement, what to follow or what to avoid.

177
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:07,720
But now, with all the knowledge we've just learned, I believe you

178
00:17:07,720 --> 00:17:11,079
won't have much trouble finding out what needs to be improved.

179
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:16,940
However, to not get lost in analyzing questions and not know where

180
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:22,180
to begin, let me remind you of the guiding questions to evaluate

181
00:17:22,180 --> 00:17:26,000
task achievement and coherence and cohesion.

182
00:17:27,079 --> 00:17:30,500
One, is the sample divided into paragraphs?

183
00:17:31,440 --> 00:17:33,960
Two, are they complete paragraphs?

184
00:17:34,880 --> 00:17:40,400
Three, is there an overview and does it play its role correctly?

185
00:17:41,380 --> 00:17:46,240
Four, does the sample provide all important details and data?

186
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:53,540
Five, is there cohesion within sentences and between paragraphs and

187
00:17:53,540 --> 00:17:55,760
are cohesive devices used correctly?

188
00:17:56,720 --> 00:18:00,880
Please pause the video to analyze the sample effectively.

189
00:18:01,340 --> 00:18:03,200
I'll be here waiting for you.

190
00:18:09,670 --> 00:18:15,450
About paragraphing, this sample is quite well divided with three parts,

191
00:18:15,630 --> 00:18:20,710
including introduction and overview, body 1 and body 2.

192
00:18:21,410 --> 00:18:27,130
Each paragraph includes at least two sentences, avoiding the paragraphing mistake, 

193
00:18:27,130 --> 00:18:31,030
which was seen in the previous band 5 samples.

194
00:18:31,870 --> 00:18:35,730
However, please read the overview more carefully.

195
00:18:36,570 --> 00:18:38,670
We can see a big problem.

196
00:18:39,450 --> 00:18:43,770
The overview does not play its role correctly, which is providing 

197
00:18:43,770 --> 00:18:47,170
a summary of the most noticeable features in the maps.

198
00:18:47,750 --> 00:18:51,970
There are many changes and it is too general and doesn't help

199
00:18:51,970 --> 00:18:54,830
readers understand what to focus on.

200
00:18:55,710 --> 00:19:01,810
Overviews like many changes, many differences, or changed a lot, 

201
00:19:01,810 --> 00:19:05,350
are not effective and might not be counted in the word count.

202
00:19:05,990 --> 00:19:08,690
So, it's best to avoid writing like this.

203
00:19:09,790 --> 00:19:16,190
Without a proper overview, this sample falls straight to band 5 in task achievement.

204
00:19:17,390 --> 00:19:22,830
For the question, does the sample provide all important details and data?

205
00:19:23,390 --> 00:19:30,870
The sample has two paragraphs, one describing 1986 and the other focusing on the present.

206
00:19:31,690 --> 00:19:35,870
This structure isn't the best, but it isn't a big problem.

207
00:19:36,650 --> 00:19:40,350
The real problem is how the information is presented.

208
00:19:41,510 --> 00:19:47,490
Too many details were listed, including the unnecessary data, like 

209
00:19:47,490 --> 00:19:50,250
the number of houses, which is not important.

210
00:19:51,150 --> 00:19:56,590
This makes the sample extend to over 200 words, much longer than

211
00:19:56,590 --> 00:20:04,110
it should be for task 1, and lacks focus, leading to losing task achievement scores.

212
00:20:04,790 --> 00:20:09,810
Additionally, the cohesion between sentences is weak, with almost no

213
00:20:09,810 --> 00:20:15,590
connections at all, and the information is disorganized, lacking a logical flow, 

214
00:20:15,590 --> 00:20:19,770
leading to losing coherence and cohesion scores.

215
00:20:20,470 --> 00:20:24,710
It's important to be aware of these errors so that you can

216
00:20:24,710 --> 00:20:27,270
avoid making them in your own writing.

217
00:20:31,590 --> 00:20:33,690
Great job reaching the end of the lesson.

218
00:20:34,070 --> 00:20:36,690
I hope maps feel much less intimidating now.

219
00:20:37,310 --> 00:20:39,490
Let's quickly recap what we covered.

220
00:20:39,810 --> 00:20:45,070
We learned the big three, checking the what, where, and when to get our verb tensors right.

221
00:20:45,450 --> 00:20:50,030
We used the great three to find global features like changes in

222
00:20:50,030 --> 00:20:53,170
area or infrastructure for our overview.

223
00:20:53,790 --> 00:20:58,590
And finally, we used the major three to group our details logically.

224
00:20:59,290 --> 00:21:03,950
Remember, the key to a high score isn't listing every single tiny detail, 

225
00:21:03,950 --> 00:21:08,070
but organizing the major changes clearly.

226
00:21:08,070 --> 00:21:09,890
See you in the next lesson!

